<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ur-Nanshe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ur-Nanshe of Lagash</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ur-Nanshe was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> around 2500 BCE. His inscriptions record the building of temples and canals, and one notes that ships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> brought him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> from foreign lands. Carved limestone plaques from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> show him carrying a basket of earth for a temple foundation, surrounded by his family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ur-nanshe.jpg" alt="Ur-Nanshe" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ur-Nanshe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ur-Nanshe, also called Ur-Nina, was the first king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> in the Sumerian Early Dynastic Period III. He was probably not of royal lineage, being the son of Gunidu, who is recorded without a royal title, and his inscriptions name Gurmu as his grandfather. He came to power after Lugalshaengur, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensi">ensi</a>, or high priest, of Lagash, who is known only from the macehead inscription of Mesilim. He was the father of Akurgal, who succeeded him, and the grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a>, who defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>, as shown on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a>, and continued his building work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ur-Nanshe is known through inscriptions to have commissioned many building projects, including canals and temples, and to have defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> against its rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>, whose ensi Pabilgagaltuku he recorded capturing. The Perforated Relief of King Ur-Nanshe, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, attributes to him the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>, the temple of Nanshe in Nina and Apsubanda. A door socket in the Louvre adds the houses of Gatumdug and Ninmar, a harem, the Ibgal and the Kinir. The Ibgal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> lies at al-Hiba, the ancient city of Lagash, where an oval wall surrounds the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> temple on the southwest edge of the city, an unusual position because Sumerian temples were usually central.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Perforated Relief is a limestone slab with two registers. In the upper one Ur-Nanshe wears a kaunakes, a tufted wool skirt, and carries a basket of bricks on his head while his wife or daughter and four of his sons stand around him. In the lower one he sits at a banquet celebrating the building of the temple, with officials and three more sons, and he is drawn much larger than everyone else. An inscription in front of him reads that boats from the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> carried wood to him, the oldest known written record of Dilmun and of imports into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. A hymn to Nanshe says that she seated Ur-Nanshe, the beloved lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, on the throne.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ur-Nanshe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaic tablets from Uruk of the late fourth millennium BCE already mention it, and <b>Ur-Nanshe of Lagash</b> (c. 2500 BCE) recorded that the ships of Dilmun brought him timber from foreign lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ur-Nanshe</b>, 'son of Gu-NI.DU', left many inscriptions, nearly all about building temples; one records a channel dug against the Amorites, and several say he had the ships of Dilmun bring timber as tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
